the gulf of gokova in southwest turkey  is renowned for its marine biodiversity  one part of the bay is a breeding ground  for sandbar sharks an endangered species  but invasive species like rabbit fish  are a growing threat to the fragile  local ecosystem  rabbit fish as an individual it doesn't  tread any other fish but entire  ecosystem once they graze the  all macroalgae it's all barren we lose  so much biodiversity because other  fishes larvaes or other small juveniles  you know invertebrates small shrimps  shells they're all hiding living under  this microalgae  a further problem besetting the gulf of  gokova is overfishing  samiha barshak's husband died over 10  years ago since then she's had to earn  her own living  but she's up against big fishing  trawlers  they don't leave me anything they come  here with their great big boats and take  everything  20 years ago i used to catch 20 to 30  kilos of fish now i'm happy if i net two  to three kilos  six areas of the bay are now protected  fishing is forbidden  the zones are maintained by a turkish  ngo called the mediterranean  conservation society  zafar kizil kaya counts the fish in the  protected areas at regular intervals  also calculating their biomass to see  whether stocks have been replenished  since the fishing ban was introduced  the results show us exactly what we  towed  this is has this area has the higher  fish biomass  so it's approximately 60 grams per  square meter this is more than you know  six times more  more  than the beginning  welcome on board  the ngo has also come up with another  project to boost the livelihoods of  local fishermen and women  they now offer excursions in their  fishing vessels to tourists  and they take a cut of the proceeds  they want to be on the water they want  to be on the sea on the boat and this is  what they are doing  and they are making money  showing their cultural life  and you know  some interesting part of fishing in the  gerkawa bay to tourists  and once the fishermen and women no  longer depend on their catches to earn  their living the bay's biodiversity has  a better chance of surviving
 

